Q: How to Extend the Type Class A: <p>With C# 14, you can declare extension members that extend the <code>type</code>s.</p>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/whats-new/csharp-14#extension-members" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/whats-new/csharp-14#extension-members</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Please I want to Extend Type Class by IsBuiltIn new method</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is how you extend the <code>Type</code> class using C# 14 syntax</p>
<pre><code>
public static class MyExtentions
{
    extension(Type)
    {
        public static bool IsBuiltIn(string _type)
        {
            return (Type.GetType(_type) == null) ? false : true;
        }
    }         
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage:</p>
<pre><code>bool ch = Type.IsBuiltIn(&quot;System.Int32&quot;);  
</code></pre>

Q: Need to disable the submit button if both fields are invalid A: <p>You may review your concept. If a user provided invalid input, then you can</p>
<ul>
<li>Disable the <code>submit</code> button</li>
<li>Invalidate the user input on Submit</li>
<li>Do both, i.e. disable the <code>submit</code> button <strong>and</strong> invalidate the input (might be an overkill)</li>
</ul>
<p>If you just disable the <code>submit</code> button, then the user has no information why the input fails and where the problem is. Usually the user will not check the output of the console. You would need extra code to provide such information to the user.</p>
<p>Your code has another issue, you check the input values based on the response of an AJAX request which are asynchronous. The user may enter an invalid value and immediately click the <code>submit</code> button before your server returns 'taken' and submit button becomes disabled. So, you need to check the input value at <code>submit</code> anyway which raise the question: why do you check the input twice?</p>
<p>You could make a synchronous call <code>await $.ajax({...})</code> (option <code>async: false</code> is deprecated or even removed already, so better don't use it) to avoid this situation, but then you would need to call the server and wait(!) for response after every single character the user enters which is not very user-friendly.</p>
<p>So, I would suggest to use standard custom validation like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>
$( &quot;form&quot; ).on( &quot;submit&quot;, function( event ) {
     let valid = true;
     await $.ajax({ ...
        if (cleanedResponse === 'taken') {
            $thisField.addClass('is-invalid'); // optional
            $thisField.get(0).setCustomValidity('duplicate ip address'); // pseudo class :invalid is added automatically
            valid = false;
        } else {
            $thisField.removeClass('is-invalid'); // most likely useless, because a new page is loaded instantly 
            $thisField.get(0).setCustomValidity(''); // empty string! Not `null` nor `undefined` nor `false`
        }

     })
     return valid; 
});

</code></pre>
<p>If you like to disable the <code>submit</code> button on top of it, you can do so but I don't see any reason for it. It just requires more code.</p>
<p>Notes: It does not make any sense to disable the <code>submit</code> button inside the <code>submit</code> handler, because when this function runs, then the user had already clicked the <code>submit</code> button when it was still enabled.</p>

Q: Control the compression level when writing Parquet files using Polars in Rust A: <p>It is possible now.</p>
<p>The docs for this module: <a href="https://docs.pola.rs/api/rust/dev/polars/prelude/enum.ParquetCompression.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.pola.rs/api/rust/dev/polars/prelude/enum.ParquetCompression.html</a></p>
